Fresh issue observation: {"body": "Legacy identity: `FOS-000005`. Original source: [immutable historical definition](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/73f891d6c0cf498ac92e804d7d0f0cd344a083f5/local-docs/reviews/observability-sweep.md).\n\nShared requirements and grouping: https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/8. All original criteria below remain required. Source status statements below describe their historical revision; current delivery evidence is recorded separately. Original relative links are resolved below.\n\n## Original definition (verbatim)\n\n5. **FOS-000005 — Separate replay-thread work completion from item success.** **M; depends on `FOS-000001` and `FOS-000004` for complete propagation; §2–§3.** [worker:1800](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_replay.cpp#L1800) discards the parse return and increments `completed_count` after processing; creation helpers return void. [normal replay:4553](https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/blob/556da9ff7d7616704dda515756219ab1dab693ec/cli/fossilize_replay.cpp#L4553) returns success after the queue finishes (E7). Introduce a thread-safe outcome accumulator independent of optional shared memory. First cover parse/read and Vulkan creation failures, then setup/validation/support/dependency exits. Propagate the result through normal replay, child and controller. **Check:** E7, missing entry, injected Vulkan failure, and mixed success/failure. Keep queue progress as queue progress. Unsupported required work must be unsuccessful under the declared policy, even when it is not a crash.\n\n   **Required automated success criteria — pending implementation:**\n\n   - **Replay unit and CLI integration tests:** E7, missing input entries, injected creation errors, unsupported required work, validation failures and dependency-blocked work produce the expected distinct outcomes and nonzero aggregate exits. A fully successful workload returns zero.\n   - **Thread/mode matrix:** The same fixed mixed workload has the same final logical outcome in normal, child/controller and progress modes, with shared progress absent/present and one/multiple threads. Queue completion still reaches its expected count even when processing fails; it is never the success oracle.\n\n<!-- fossilize-migration-v1:FOS-000005 -->\n", "id": "I_kwDOVZpjbc8AAAABamKdBA", "number": 14, "state": "OPEN", "title": "FOS-000005 — Separate replay-thread work completion from item success", "url": "https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/14"}

Fresh issue observation: {"body": "Required outcome: deliver every migrated observability child with its original criteria. This epic stays open until all required children and shared gates complete.\n\n## Ordered implementation backlog\n\nAll criteria below are mandatory completion gates for the applicable implementation slice. They describe future assertions, not tests already implemented or passing. A task is complete only when its criteria are covered by committed automated tests registered with the project test runner/CI, and passing evidence records the commands, configurations and platforms. If a task is split, each split-out item must receive its own registered key and name its covered criteria; the parent remains incomplete until all are satisfied. Manual inspection, log screenshots and the observation-only research runner are insufficient acceptance evidence.\n\nUse unit tests for result/state logic and deterministic fault injection, plus subprocess/filesystem/IPC/layer integration tests for externally visible behavior. Assert returned outcomes, exit statuses, structured fields, actual output contents and handle/application behavior; prose-only assertions are appropriate only where human presentation itself is the requirement. Include successful controls alongside failure cases. Upgrade E1–E9 into assertion-bearing regressions as their owning tasks are implemented. Tests must expose the defect before its fix where feasible, rather than merely mirror implementation internals.\n\nConcurrency/deadline tests must use fake clocks, barriers and controllable adapters instead of random driver instability or fragile sleeps. Give process tests watchdogs and isolated temporary output directories. Linux and Windows process changes require platform-specific automated evidence; GPU-independent mocks are the baseline, with hardware jobs for hardware claims. An unavailable platform/device is skipped/unverified and leaves that acceptance criterion open. Record applicable debug/release and exception-enabled/disabled coverage; use sanitizer jobs where useful for changed parsing, lifetime or concurrency code. Tests must check honest unknown outcomes where evidence is deliberately lost.\n\n**Compatibility gate for every affected public boundary:** declare whether source, binary, protocol and behavioral compatibility are preserved or intentionally changed. Maintain compile/run fixtures for preserved old callers/invocations, schema/protocol fixtures for preserved consumers and prebuilt-consumer checks only where binary compatibility is claimed. Assert and document intentional changes, including formerly false-success CLI exits. Additive signatures alone do not establish compatibility; do not claim that all existing callers continue working. Require structured outcomes without prose parsing once the relevant result API/mode is introduced.\n\n\n## Dependency and delivery notes\n\nThe critical path is **FOS-000005 → FOS-000009 → (FOS-000010 and FOS-000011) → FOS-000012 → FOS-000013 → FOS-000014**: worker facts precede reconciliation, which precedes a trustworthy controller/API result. **FOS-000003 → FOS-000016 → FOS-000017 → FOS-000019** is the persistence/capture path. **FOS-000006** prevents later runs from trusting a failed attempt. **FOS-000004** is an early containment fix, not a substitute for the richer accounting. Initial result schemas may report persistence/support detail as unknown until the respective producers are migrated; they must not imply whole-standard compliance early.\n\nKeep regression verification alongside every iteration. Do not defer all tests to `FOS-000024`. The highest-value fixtures are E1–E9, deterministic worker failure/deadline injection, duplicate/stale messages, full diagnostic rings, close-time ENOSPC, optional versus required output loss, and cancellation before/after final publication. Linux and Windows process implementations need the same behavioral matrix even when mechanisms differ. Apple/Android builds use `NO_ROBUST_REPLAYER`; ordinary replay/library/capture contracts still apply without inventing worker support there.\n\n\n## Remaining verification\n\nBefore implementation is accepted, run supported CMake builds and tests on Linux and Windows, exception-enabled/disabled and release/debug configurations as applicable. Add deterministic fault injection rather than relying solely on the existing random `SIMULATE_UNSTABLE_DRIVER` hooks. Exercise runtime capture with multiple instances/devices and real Vulkan support failures; test cache feedback on hardware that exposes it. No current probe demonstrates actual cache warming or durable driver output.\n\n\n## Unkeyed report disposition\n# Repeated shader processing with unclear failure and cache-reuse outcomes\n\nThe Sims 4 repeatedly delayed startup with “Processing Vulkan shaders,” despite previous processing having finished.\n\nSteam launched Fossilize’s replayer before Proton, with a **10-second limit without pipeline completion**. Controlled tests showed workers timing out and compilation being interrupted. Allowing 60 seconds let the tested workload finish; its resulting cache was reusable.\n\nThe investigation was complicated by several observability gaps:\n\n- **Hidden failure context:** Steam’s normal logs showed progress and completion without an actionable explanation of worker failures. Separate, unsuppressed replay runs exposed the timeouts.\n- **Ambiguous crash reporting:** Timeouts used SIGABRT and the crash-recovery path. A debugger was needed to establish that an interrupted worker was executing inside NVIDIA’s shader compiler; this did not prove a spontaneous driver crash.\n- **Unclear completion:** One replay returned exit status zero despite reporting “Cannot continue,” making successful termination insufficient evidence of completed work.\n- **Unclear reuse:** The logs did not clearly connect replay triggers, previously processed entries, skipped/failed work and persisted results. A shader-bucket update also triggered processing of an unchanged generic database.\n\n**The integration gap:** Fossilize needs to expose explicit failure reasons and partial outcomes even in quiet mode. Steam needs to retain and present those results alongside why replay was scheduled and what reuse information is available.\n\nThe investigation did **not** establish that the entire cache was discarded. Cache reuse worked in controlled tests, while deliberately forcing a warm replay still incurred substantial processing overhead.\n\n## References\n\nInvestigated 26–27 October 2026.\n\n- [Upstream issue: steam-for-linux #13810](https://github.com/ValveSoftware/steam-for-linux/issues/13810)\n- [Sanitized supporting evidence](https://gist.github.com/robinwassen/6d8cd12e4040cf409cb3553f4fb87b61)\n\nFossilize obligations overlap FOS-000004/000005/000007/000009/000012/000013/000014/000022; retained individually without a duplicate issue. Steam scheduling/presentation belongs to external steam-for-linux#13810; no new Fossilize implementation requirement.\n\nSpecifications, reproduced E1..E9 findings, adversarial review and sanitized fixtures are supporting evidence, not additional task definitions.\n\n<!-- fossilize-migration-v1:observability -->\n", "id": "I_kwDOVZpjbc8AAAABamD6Iw", "number": 8, "state": "OPEN", "title": "Observability: preserve trustworthy outcomes across Fossilize", "url": "https://github.com/robinwassen/Fossilize/issues/8"}

